# {{AGENT_NAME}} — Buildkite Build Agent (operations)

Watches Buildkite builds of this repo and turns failed ones into grounded
GitHub triage issues. Event-driven — no cron, no servers to maintain.
Installed by
[ai-automations](https://github.com/makasanaakshay/ai-automation-master);
re-run its installer to update (answers saved in `automation.config.json`).

```
Buildkite build.failed webhook (X-Buildkite-Signature HMAC verified, X-Buildkite-Token fallback)
  → Cloudflare Worker relay            scripts/buildkite-agent/relay/worker.js
  → repository_dispatch                .github/workflows/buildkite-agent.yml
  → agent-run.sh                       restore state → resolve → job-log tail → playbook → save state
```

| Trigger | Playbook | Output (GitHub only — Buildkite is never written) |
|---|---|---|
| A build ends in state `failed` | `analyze` | ONE GitHub issue labeled `{{ISSUE_LABEL}}`: what failed, log excerpt, likely cause (code-verified), where to look, links — or ONE comment when the same commit sha already has an open issue |

State lives on the orphan branch `buildkite-agent-state`
(`state/<build_number>.json`: phase, gh_issue_url, commit_sha). Every run
re-fetches truth from Buildkite's API and diffs against it — webhook payloads
are only doorbells. Dedupe is **sha-search based**, not state-keyed: state
only prevents reprocessing the same build number (retriggers, duplicate
deliveries); a NEW failed build of an already-failed commit is caught by the
playbook searching open `{{ISSUE_LABEL}}` issues for the commit sha and
commenting instead of filing. The failing job's log tail (~8000 chars,
ANSI-stripped) is fetched up front and inlined into the prompt, so triage
works even when the failure predates the run. Issue bodies and comments end
with the `{{AGENT_MARKER}}` footer.

## Buildkite delivery auth (two schemes, honestly)

The webhook service's Token authenticates deliveries two ways: an
`X-Buildkite-Signature` header (`timestamp=<t>,signature=<hex>` — hex
HMAC-SHA256 of `<t>.<body>`, keyed with the token) and the older
`X-Buildkite-Token` header carrying the token in plain. The relay verifies
the signature whenever that header is present — it proves the body wasn't
tampered with — and falls back to comparing the token header otherwise,
which only proves the sender knows the token. Either passing accepts the
delivery; both absent or wrong is a 401.

## Operating

- **Recover / backfill:** Actions → *Buildkite Build Agent* → *Run workflow* — empty `item_id` reconciles the 10 most recent failed builds (`state=failed` on the configured pipeline) + tracked ones; a build number processes one.
- **Transcripts:** every run uploads `.agent-out/` as an artifact (prompt, job-log context, stream).
- **Model:** `AI_MODEL` in `scripts/buildkite-agent/env.sh` (currently `{{AI_MODEL}}`, brain `{{BRAIN_NAME}}`).
- **Pause:** delete the webhook notification service (or drop `build.failed` from its events) in Buildkite Organization Settings → Notification Services — the relay can stay deployed; recreating the service mints a new Token (`wrangler secret put BUILDKITE_WEBHOOK_SECRET` again).
- **Known blind spot:** builds that fail while the webhook is down are only caught by the reconcile button — it scans the last 10 failed builds.

## Local dry-run (safe — needs BUILDKITE_TOKEN exported)

```bash
DRY_RUN=1 bash scripts/buildkite-agent/agent-run.sh                 # reconcile scan
DRY_RUN=1 ITEM_ID=<build_number> bash scripts/buildkite-agent/agent-run.sh
```

## Guardrails

- ONE GitHub issue per failing commit sha — new failed builds of the same commit become comments on it; closed issues are left closed.
- Buildkite access is READ-ONLY — the agent never retries, cancels, or rebuilds a build; humans own the CI pipeline.
- No secrets from CI logs: env values, tokens, and connection strings that leak into logs are scrubbed before anything reaches GitHub.
- Never writes code — git stays clean apart from the state branch.
- `--dangerously-skip-permissions` only inside the disposable CI runner; local runs are `DRY_RUN` and stop before any AI call.
